  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 215 AM EST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nBy Friday afternoon and evening the low pressure system will \ncontinue to be situated over the great lakes region putting our area \nto the south. Another area of low pressure will round the base of \nthe trough on Friday before being absorbed into the main flow which \nwill result in another frontal boundary pushing into Georgia. \nCurrent model guidance points towards limited rain chances (20% or \nless) with this system with PWATs around 1 inch forecasted \ncurrently. A jet streak is forecasted to be directly over north \nGeorgia with this flow which could aid in limited rain chances being \nsqueezed out. Overall rain chances remain limited with the main \nimpact into the weekend being colder air being pushed into north \nGeorgia behind this front. Temperatures will decrease into the 30s \novernight beginning as early as Saturday morning and lasting through \nat least Tuesday morning. High temps will also lower into the mid to \nupper 60s this weekend. Past this weekend there remains much \nuncertainty with rain chances. While the GFS is indicating an \nincrease in rain chances for Monday and potentially Tuesday with a low \npressure system pushing eastward across the SE, the ECMWF is not \nbuying into this solution. Will need to keep an eye on these two \nsolutions as this time frame is likely to be our next chance for \nrainfall.",
